# competitive.cfg - the netcode spec a CoDUO+ SERVER pushes to CoDUO+ clients.
#
# Copy this file as competitive.cfg next to mss32.dll on the machine that runs the
# server (CoDUOMP.exe dedicated, or a listen server). The mod publishes it through
# systeminfo (sv_competitive, sv_competitive2..4); CoDUO+ clients apply it while they
# are connected and release it when they leave. Vanilla clients ignore it.
# The file is re-read every second: edit it live, no restart.
#
# Format, one rule per line, comments on their OWN line:
#   cvar value         locked on the client (read-only while connected)
#   cvar min max       clamped into [min, max] (the player chooses inside)
# Only letters, digits and _ . + - in names and values.
#
# Pair it with the SERVER settings (server.cfg / command line), which it cannot set:
#   set sv_fps 40          snapshots only go out on server frames - see "snaps"
#   set sv_maxRate 0       0 = no server cap; otherwise >= 90000 or "rate" below is cut
# Full reasoning: docs/NETCODE.md.
#
# KEEP IT SHORT. The spec travels in the server's systeminfo string, which the engine
# caps at 1024 characters - and a stock UO server already overflows it (with many map
# pk3s, sv_paks / sv_referencedPaks and several ui_* vote settings are dropped from what
# clients receive, mod or not). Every character here pushes another vanilla key out, so
# only the four lines that 40-tick needs are active; the rest are commented examples.

# ---- snapshots: MUST equal sv_fps ------------------------------------------------
# The server sends a snapshot when nextSnapshotTime = now + 1000/snaps has passed, but
# only on a server frame. snaps lower than sv_fps skips frames; higher gains nothing.
# 40 needs the CoDUO+ server (vanilla caps snaps at 30, CoDUO+ lifts it to 40).
snaps 40

# ---- client -> server packets -----------------------------------------------------
# 125 = the CoDUO+ cap. One packet per client frame at most, sent when 1000/125 = 8 ms
# have passed: exactly 125 pps at com_maxfps 125 or 250 (with CoDUO+'s frame limiter).
cl_maxpackets 125
# 125 / 250 are the frame rates whose period divides 8 ms; other values in between
# (e.g. 200) still work but send fewer packets (200 fps -> 100 pps). 333 is allowed for
# its movement physics: exact 3 ms frames, a packet every third one (111 pps).
com_maxfps 125 333

# ---- bandwidth ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The next snapshot waits (size + 48) * 1000 / rate ms (SV_RateMsec, sizes counted up
# to 1500). A full snapshot is ~1548 B: 40/s needs ~62 kB/s in a busy fight, and the
# engine's own ceiling is 90000. Lower rates delay snapshots exactly when it matters.
rate 90000
# delta compression keeps snapshots small (it is also what keeps them under the rate)
# cl_nodelta 0

# ---- loss and interpolation -----------------------------------------------------
# each packet repeats the previous 1 (to 2) commands: a lost packet loses no input
# cl_packetdup 1 2
# negative = render closer to the newest snapshot (less lead to aim with, a little
# extrapolation); positive would only add delay
# cl_timenudge -20 0
